## What this session established

The previous session ended flagging one unverified question before boarding step 4a: *does mount-time recovery take an AG DLM lock?* It does, and settling that turned up a **bootstrap deadlock in step 4a as shipped**. I held the rig run rather than watching a mount hang.

**The inversion.** `xfs_mountfs` sits between mount step 6.5 (which now *records* stale foreign slots instead of purging them) and the post-mountfs settle (the only thing that ever fences/replays/releases those grants). But `xfs_log_mount_finish` → intent replay and iunlink processing take blocking cluster locks — `xfs_free_extent` at `xfs/libxfs/xfs_alloc.c:4868` is an unconditional blocking AG EX, and `__mxfs_ag_dlm_lock` (`xfs/xfs_mxfs_dlm.c:34101`) has no phase guard. Two failures follow:

1. A frozen foreign bit isn't heartbeating, so the CAW liveness extension doesn't apply: the wait breaks at 120 s with `-ETIMEDOUT`, which fails `xlog_recover_finish` → fails the mount → the settle is never reached. Circular — our recovery needs the grant released, releasing it needs the foreign slice replayed, replaying needs a mounted FS.
2. Worse at 32 nodes: step 4 keeps each node's *own* retained EX/PW bits across its entire `xfs_mountfs`. In a mount storm every node is heartbeating (so `holders_alive` is TRUE and the wait extends to the 480 s hard cap) while holding retained bits — A blocking on B's while B blocks on A's is a genuine distributed deadlock.

Pre-4a this couldn't happen; 6.5 purged those bits, and its own comment named the root_dir case it existed to prevent.

**GPT's ruling (RULE 5):** hold the board; restructure as a pre-`xfs_mountfs` recovery barrier. It rejected the two cheaper options and gave one load-bearing reassurance — per-node LSNs give no global replay order, so correctness has to come from force-before-conflicting-lock-transfer, and MXFS *has* that (Architectural Invariant 1). The foundation is sound; only the mount ordering is wrong.

**The seam is verified.** `mxfs_xlog_recover_foreign_slice` (`xfs/xfs_log.c:742`) needs only `mp->m_log` and a private shadow xlog — no root inode, no live AIL, no transactions. So the barrier goes in `xfs/xfs_mount.c` right after the `xfs_log_mount()` error check at line 1057, before anything takes a cluster lock. Running settle phases 1–2 there too (rather than after `xfs_mountfs`) is what kills failure mode 2: the retained bits only ever gated image replay inside `xfs_log_mount`.

Two things keep the cost acceptable: `mxfs_disklock_confirm_dead_mask` early-exits when its candidate set empties, so a concurrent mount storm drops peers within a heartbeat interval and only a genuinely dead slot costs the full 62 s; and CAW's single-node fast path means a node mounting alone never blocks at all.

The full design, the new v5 API split, and one open question GPT raised (intent replay and iunlink need the same authority treatment as buffer-image replay — not yet analysed) are in ccmemory as `ccloop-c7ee71c6-sess55-step4a-mount-ordering-inversion-GPT-ruling`.

**Criteria: NO.** Not production ready. 11 open defects, 4 critical; defect #1's step-4a edits are landed but now known-defective in their mount ordering, unboarded, and unverified.

